# =========================================================================
# server.bcyr — benchmarks for the HTTP request path.
#
# No oracle bench file: `rust-old/benches/` has no server harness, because the
# Rust request path is axum's and benchmarking it would have measured tokio.
# The port's path is `agnosai_route_dispatch`, which is ordinary synchronous
# code, so it can be timed directly.
#
# ## What these exist to prove
#
# B2 threads the per-request arena through the read routes, and
# `tests/server_serve.tcyr` proves the **memory** claim — dispatch through
# `dispatch_a` charges the global bump nothing beyond the router's match
# struct. That measurement says nothing about **time**, and threading is not
# free by construction: every `_a` call carries an extra argument, and
# `alloc_via` is one indirection more than `alloc`.
#
# So each threaded route is timed on both arms. The paired rows are the claim:
# threading buys the memory without costing latency. A regression in either
# direction shows up as one row moving against the other.
#
# ## Why the global arm is the slower one anyway
#
# The global allocator is a no-free bump: a request's garbage is never
# reclaimed, so a long-lived process walks an ever-growing heap and loses cache
# locality that the arena — reset to its base between requests — keeps. The
# arena arm pays one `reset_via` per iteration, which is a pointer store.
#
# `route_resolve` is timed on its own because it is the residual: the match
# struct is minted before dispatch reaches any arm, so it is in **both** numbers
# and neither route threading nor any further handler work can remove it.
#
# It is the residual **only for the `/api/v1` rows**. There are two ladders:
# `/api/v1/...` enters the prefix branch (`router.cyr:293`) and walks its group
# chain, while `/health`, `/ready` and `/metrics` miss that prefix entirely and
# are matched as bare literals below it (`router.cyr:402`). `route_resolve_probe`
# is the probe rows' residual, and it is measured rather than assumed.
#
# ## What the timed region does NOT include
#
# `agnosai_route_dispatch` stops at the response struct. For a JSON route that
# struct only **points at** the value tree (`agnosai_route_json_a`,
# `routes/mod.cyr:97-108`); the bytes are rendered afterwards by
# `bayan_json_v_build_a` in `_agnosai_serve_send` (`serve.cyr:433-434`), which is
# outside every row in this file. `/metrics` is the one exception:
# `agnosai_route_metrics` (`routes/health.cyr:55-56`) hands
# `agnosai_metrics_gather`'s finished `Str` to `agnosai_route_text`, so that row
# alone carries its whole body render.
#
# ✅ **CLOSED 2026-08-13 (roadmap D5): the body IS rendered inside the timed
# region now.** Every dispatch row calls `_b_render_body` / `_b_render_body_a`
# on the result, reproducing `_agnosai_serve_send`'s branch set exactly — text
# routes render nothing extra, JSON routes build their value tree to bytes, and
# the null/204 arms are guarded the same way. So a JSON row is now the WHOLE
# reply: body-limit check, resolve, auth, handler, value tree, **and
# serialisation** — which is what `rust-old/benches/server.rs:43-62` always
# measured, since axum's `Json` `IntoResponse` serialises inside the stack
# `app.oneshot` drove. A serialisation regression is visible here for the first
# time.
#
# ⚠ **This RE-BASELINED every JSON row in this file on 2026-08-13.** Rows are not
# comparable across that date. `route_metrics_global` is unaffected — it already
# carried its whole body render, since `agnosai_route_metrics` hands
# `agnosai_metrics_gather`'s finished `Str` to `agnosai_route_text` — and it is
# now genuinely comparable with its siblings rather than a measurement-class
# outlier.
#
# ## Three rows here are deliberately SINGLE-arm, and that is not an omission
#
# `/health`, `/ready` and `/metrics` have no `_a` handler, and
# `_agnosai_route_dispatch_inner` calls the bare form unconditionally
# (`router.cyr:525-527`). An arena twin would time the 24-byte match struct and
# nothing else — `route_resolve_probe` already isolates exactly that for these
# three paths — so publishing the pair would make the paired-arm claim above
# false for those rows.
#
# ## The prompt_guard family lives here, not in orch.bcyr
#
# `benches/orch.bcyr` already carries `prompt_scan_clean_67b` and
# `prompt_scan_clean_4k`, so the 500-byte family could have gone there. It is
# here because the whole value of the suspicious and sanitize rows is the delta
# against a clean scan **at the same length**, and a delta a reader is expected
# to perform is one both halves must be printed for, in one run, on one host.
# All three 500-byte rows are therefore in one file, self-subtracting. `main`
# sets `SK_FATAL`, which sanitize's warn path (`prompt_guard.cyr:174`) needs
# suppressed anyway.
#
# That is not an argument against ever reading across files, and the sizing note
# on `_b_prompt_scan_clean_500` does exactly that: checking a fresh row against
# the O(len) slope of two committed ones is a one-off design check made against
# `bench-history.csv`, not a subtraction this file asks its reader to redo.
#
# These are CYRIUS baseline numbers, NOT comparable to
# rust-old/bench-history.csv. See CLAUDE.md.
#
# Stdlib is auto-prepended from cyrius.cyml — do NOT include stdlib modules.
# =========================================================================

